faq:
  - q: "How do I find a job with no work experience?"
    a: "Three things convert without work experience: (1) Relevant internships, projects, or coursework that demonstrate applied skills — not just GPA. A personal project, a university consulting engagement, a campus organization leadership role all count as evidence. (2) A specific target — new grads who apply broadly get ignored; new grads who target a specific function (product management, data analytics, software engineering) with tailored positioning get interviews. (3) Network activation — professors, internship supervisors, family contacts, alumni — the people who will refer you are your fastest path to a first interview."
  - q: "What should a new grad resume look like?"
    a: "One page, outcome-focused wherever possible. Lead with education (until you have 2+ years of work experience). Internships and co-ops should list outcomes, not just responsibilities. Include a projects section for coursework, personal projects, and hackathons — especially for technical roles. Skills section should list all relevant technical skills. Remove high school content after your first year in college. The most common new grad resume mistake: listing what you did, not what you produced."
  - q: "How do I negotiate salary as a new grad?"
    a: "Research first: Glassdoor, LinkedIn Salary, and talking to peers and recent alumni in similar roles gives you a market range. Most new grad offers are negotiable — entry-level salary bands typically have 5–15% flexibility. The ask: 'Thank you for the offer. I'm very excited about this role. Based on my research and [specific factor — location, technical skills, internship background], I was expecting something closer to [target]. Is there flexibility?' For structured programs (rotational programs, investment banking, FAANG), comp is typically fixed — negotiate signing bonus instead."
  - q: "Should I go to graduate school or get a job first?"
    a: "Work first for most fields. Graduate school is most valuable when: (1) the degree is required for the career path (law, medicine, clinical psychology); (2) you want to change fields in a way that requires credentials (MBA for career pivots, MS for technical transitions); or (3) you have a specific research goal. For most careers, 2–3 years of work experience makes graduate school more valuable — you know what you want, you qualify for better programs, and you can often get your employer to pay for it."
  - q: "What is the fastest way to land a job after graduating?"
    a: "Three things produce the fastest results: (1) On-campus recruiting — companies that recruit on campus are the fastest path for new grads. Apply to on-campus programs before graduating if possible. (2) Alumni network — your university alumni network is the most underpowered asset most new grads have. Identify alumni at target companies on LinkedIn and send a brief, specific message. Shared school converts cold outreach to warm. (3) Internship conversion — the conversion rate from internship to full-time offer is the highest of any pipeline. If you had internships, those companies and contacts are the first calls to make."
